> > >
> > > or
> > >
> > > df2 <- df1
> > > df2[["x2"]] <- with(df2, x + 10)
> > >
> > > I'm guessing the motivation for transform() is to allocate memory for
> > > the whole of the new data frame in one shot, rather than
> > > incrementally?  Other than that, I can't see any benefit at all,
> > > except for creating hard to find  bugs, as you pointed out.
> >
> >
> > transform (and the plyr equivalent, mutate) are very useful when used with other functions like ddply. For example:
> >
> > df <- data.frame(g = rep(LETTERS[1:2], each=4), x = 1:8)
> > df
> > #   g x
> > # 1 A 1
> > # 2 A 2
> > # 3 A 3
> > # 4 A 4
> > # 5 B 5
> > # 6 B 6
> > # 7 B 7
> > # 8 B 8
> >
> > library(plyr)
> > ddply(df, "g", transform, norm_x = x - mean(x))
> > #   g x norm_x
> > # 1 A 1   -1.5
> > # 2 A 2   -0.5
> > # 3 A 3    0.5
> > # 4 A 4    1.5
> > # 5 B 5   -1.5
> > # 6 B 6   -0.5
> > # 7 B 7    0.5
> > # 8 B 8    1.5
> >
> >
> >
> >
> >  >> # Unfortunately, tranform cannot use newly created
> > > >> # variables in the same function call
> > > >
> > > >> df2 <- transform(df,
> > > > +                  x2 = x  + 10,
> > > > +                  x3 = x2 + 100
> > > > +                  )
> > > > Error in eval(expr, envir, enclos) : object 'x2' not found
> > > >>
> > >
> > > That one appears to be a fixable problem in the transform.data.frame
> > > method. I wonder if we couldn't fix transform. Lots of functions do
> > > use calculated variables. Example
> > >
> > >
> > > > myfn <- function(x1 , x2 = 3*x1, x4=x1*x2){
> > >    print(c(x1,x2,x4))
> > > }
> > > > myfn(x1=3)
> > > [1]  3  9 27
> > >
> > > Do you know if a wishlist item has been added in R core's transform()
> > > method for that functionality?
> >
> >
> > This would be a big change in the behavior of transform(), and I think it's unlikely that they would implement it. Here are two examples of changes:
> >
> > df <- data.frame(x = 1:4)
> >
> > # If x is modified by transform, then y would have a different value here.
> > transform(df, x = x*10, y = x+1)
> > #    x y
> > # 1 10 2
> > # 2 20 3
> > # 3 30 4
> > # 4 40 5
> >
> > # If a new column has the same name as an existing variable, any new columns
> > # that use that variable would have a different value. In this case, z would
> > # have a different value.
> > y <- 0
> > transform(df, y = x*10, z = y+1)
> > #   x  y z
> > # 1 1 10 1
> > # 2 2 20 1
> > # 3 3 30 1
